'''''Heptapleurum''''' is a [[genus]] of flowering plants in the family [[Araliaceae]], the ivy or ginseng family.  These plants are native to Southeast Asia: [[Malesia]], [[Papuasia]], southern China, Vietnam, the Indian Subcontinent, Tibet, Hainan, Taiwan, Japan, and Australia.<ref name=powo>{{cite POWO |id=3032-1 |title=''Heptapleurum'' Gaertn. |access-date=3 November 2025 }}</ref> The genus was phylogenetically resurrected from the genus ''[[Schefflera]]'' in 2020.<ref name =Lowry>{{cite journal |title=Resurrection of the Genus ''Heptapleurum'' for the Asian Clade of Species Previously Included in ''Schefflera'' (Araliaceae) |year=2020 |last1=Lowry Ii |first1=Porter P. |last2=Plunkett |first2=Gregory M. |journal=Novon: A Journal for Botanical Nomenclature |volume=28 |issue=3 |pages=143–170 |doi=10.3417/2020612 |bibcode=2020Novon..28..143L |s2cid=221860194 }}</ref> It is the largest genus of Araliaceae<ref name = Lowry/> with 321 accepted species {{as of|2025|11|lc=y}}.<ref name=powo/>

''Heptapleurum'' includes species commonly grown as houseplants or garden ornamentals (such as ''[[Heptapleurum arboricola]]'').<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/58935/Schefflera-arboricola-Gold-Capella/Details |title=''Schefflera arboricola'' 'Gold Capella' |publisher=RHS |access-date=5 March 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/pdfs/agm-lists/agm-ornamentals.pdf |title=AGM Plants - Ornamental |date=July 2017 |page=95 |publisher=Royal Horticultural Society |access-date=5 November 2018}}</ref>  Some species are threatened, endangered, or critically endangered by deforestative habitat loss and extreme endemism, such as ''[[Heptapleurum acuminatissimum]]'', which is known only from a single location with an estimated extent of occurrence of only {{cvt|4|sqkm}}.<ref name = iucn1>Energy Development Corporation (EDC), 2020. ''[https://www.iucnredlist.org/species/154179418/157178511 Schefflera acuminatissima]''. The [[IUCN Red List of Threatened Species]] 2020: e.T154179418A157178511. https://dx.doi.org/10.2305/IUCN.UK.2020-2.RLTS.T154179418A157178511.en. Accessed 7 May 2023.</ref><ref name= iucn2>{{cite iucn |author=Energy Development Corporation |collaboration=EDC |date=2020 |title=''Heptapleurum agamae'' |volume=2020 |article-number=e.T34244A153534751 |doi=10.2305/IUCN.UK.2020-2.RLTS.T34244A153534751.en |access-date=7 May 2023}}</ref><ref name=iucn3>{{cite iucn |author=World Conservation Monitoring Centre |date=1998 |title=''Heptapleurum fastigiatum'' |volume=1998 |article-number=e.T33703A9802707 |doi=10.2305/IUCN.UK.1998.RLTS.T33703A9802707.en |access-date=9 May 2023}}</ref>

They are commonly scrambling subshrubs to small trees, with many epiphytic and vining members.

